## 6. Creating and editing records

Every record type uses the same kind of form, opened from **+ New**, a page's main button, a quick
button or an **Edit** action.

- Fields marked `*` are required. If one is missing or wrong (for example an invalid email), the form
  shows the problem under the field and does not save.
- Save with the button at the bottom or `Ctrl Enter`. A message confirms "created" or "updated".
- **Links fill themselves in**: picking a contact fills in their company; picking a deal fills in its
  company and contact; changing the company clears a contact who works elsewhere.
- **Deal stage sets probability**: choosing a stage fills in its standard probability. You can still type your own.
- Forms opened from a contact, company, deal or calendar day arrive with those details already filled in.
- **Company, Contact and Deal fields are searchable.** Click the field and type part of a name. Use the
  arrow keys and `Enter`, or click. **×** clears the field.
- **Create a company on the spot**: if the company you type doesn't exist, choose
  **Create company "…"**. It's created and selected, and you can fill in its details later.
- **No duplicate companies**: when adding a company, start typing its name. Existing companies with a
  similar name or website appear under the field, so you can open the existing one instead.

| Record | Required | Other fields |
|---|---|---|
| Contact | First name, last name, email | Phone, job title, company, status, lead source, owner |
| Company | Company name | Industry, employees, website, phone, city, country, account owner |
| Deal | Deal name, amount above 0, expected close date | Stage, probability, company, contact, priority, owner |
| Task | Title, due date | Priority, assignee, status, company, contact, deal, notes |
| Activity | Subject, date and time | Type, status (Completed / Planned), company, contact, deal, owner, description |
| Ticket | Subject | Company, contact, priority, status, assignee, description |

> **Deleting:** Every delete asks for confirmation first. Deleting a record does **not** delete the
> records linked to it; they are simply unlinked. For example, deleting a company keeps its contacts,
> just without a company. The one exception: deleting a ticket also removes the notes posted on it.

